What follows is an account of Jack the neutral human tourist, who did 
recover the fabled Amulet of Yendor and returned it to the care of The Lady, 
so that she might gain ascendence over the other gods. It was also his 
guide's first ascension of a tourist, which no doubt caused him to dance 
madly around his computer with glee.

Jack descended into the dungeons poorly equipped, with no other armor than a 
t-shirt and no weapons but some enchanted darts. He did, however, have the 
wisdom to allow his pet dog to accompany him on his journey, and also had a 
fair amount of gold in his pockets. Jack descended down a few levels and let 
his dog kill everything until he found a passage to the gnomish mines spoken 
of in legend.

Descending into the mines he was confronted by hordes of gnomes. Interested 
in photo opportunities, he began taking photos. In one encounter, a gnome 
lord he took photos of was blinded and began throwing daggers, accidentally 
killing some of his own minions. The dog slew the rest, and Jack managed to 
reach a small town several levels down without bloodying his own hands. His 
pet small dog seemed to thrive on gnome corpses and soon grew to become a 
normal dog.

Despite having to grope in the darkness, Jack managed to find a general 
store and sold all his excess items for extra cash. Luckily for him, the 
town also contained a temple to The Lady, and the priestess there granted 
him divine protection for sizable donations to the temple. Jack's dog was no 
so lucky; the unfortunate beast picked a fight with a guard, who promptly 
slew it. Jack was granted sanctuary in the temple, much to the chagrin of 
the gnomes and dwarves who were gathering outside, waiting.

With no other options available, Jack begain hurling darts at the enemies, 
killing some. He managed to bring some of the corpses into the temple and 
sacrifice them. He also identified a gray stone that one of the dwarves had 
dropped as a luckstone. Hoping that The Lady would grace him with her voice, 
he continued killing monsters from the temple until he reached level 8, 
occasionally using an elvish dagger he had found. He finally became skilled 
with the dagger and, fortified with dwarven mithril, a hard helmet and iron 
shoes, tried his luck against a white unicorn and succeeded. He found that 
the horn made a good weapon.

Jack returned without a pet to the Dungeons of Doom well-protected. He 
gained skill with the unicorn horn until he reached level 6. It was there 
that he discovered a ghost hovering over a pit of poisoned spikes, guarding 
a great treasure. He dispatched the ghost of the former valkyrie and 
discovered a treasure trove, including gray dragon scale mail, a cursed bag 
of holding, a helm of telepathy, and a number of scrolls and wands. Just 
past that room was an altar to Offler, a god of chaos. He managed to convert 
the altar to The Lady's cause, and uncurse the bag using holy water. Many 
goode items lay inside, including two magic markers. He sacrified aplenty at 
this altar as well and finally The Lady spoke, proclaiming him the Envoy of 
Balance. On the altar lie a sword of great sharpness known as Vorpal Blade. 
He took the sword and, as The Lady's champion, went off to do battle.

He found another maze that branched off the main dungeon where a number of 
boulders lay strewn about. At the end was a large room of monsters, who 
guarded another bag of holding. Jack was disappointed, hoping that they 
might have been guarding an amulet.

Jack proceeded down and slew many monsters, eventually finding a large level 
covered with water with an island in the middle. Deciding not to cross 
immediately, he dug a hole down and discovered another level that looked 
just like a normal dugeon level. An altar also was on this level, and he 
converted it as well. However, he was foolish enough to try the goddess' 
patience with prayer at the altar and was punished for his impertinence. His 
divine protection was gone. Nevertheless, he was still formidably armed, and 
proceeded down until he found a great castle. The residents included several 
liches, xorns, soldiers and assorted minions. Jack slew them all and, in a 
chest in one of the towers found a coveted wand of wishing with 2 charges. 
He wished for a blessed shield of reflection and some speed boots, fixed his 
armor, and proceeded back upstairs, where use of a wand of create monster 
near the altar provided him with sacrifices to get back in to The Lady's 
good graces.

Going back up, he confronted a terrible creature whose gaze can turn men to 
stone. Seeing her own visage in Jack's shield, however, caused her to turn 
into a statue. Jack proceeded to create a moat using a wand of cold and slew 
several eels attempting to drag him under.

Back into the main dungeon, Jack found a teleporter returning him to the 
fields of his homeland. There, he was told of a foul villian named The 
Master of Thieves who had terrorized the citizens and stolen the fabled 
Platinum Yendorian Express Card. Determined not to leave home without it, 
Jack tracked the Master of Thieves to his lair and easily slew him. Invoking 
its power, he recharged his wand of wishing. Twoflowers then charged him to 
recover the Amulet of Yendor, a charge Jack dutifully accepted.

Returning to the castle, Jack fell down a trap door and wound up in the 
Valley of the Dead. He slew every beast on the level until he reached an 
altar and a priest of the foul god Moloch. He slew the priest as well, 
taking his robe. Jack went back up a few levels and made all his gear 
fireproof and well-enchanted. He felt ready to descend into the depths of 
Gehennom.

Using his magic markers, Jack wrote scrolls of magic mapping to navigate 
through the mazes, and a pick axe to chop out paths between floors. The arch 
devil Asmodeum demanded tribute, which he refused. Jack slew him as Asmodeus 
teleported back and forth from his lair to the up stairs to where Jack was 
finding his way back. Directly underneath Asmodeus' lair lay a swamp, where 
a foul demon named Juiblex assaulted Jack. Jack quickly dispatched the beast 
with Vorpal Blade, crossed the swamp using a ring of levitation he wished 
for, and proceed down. Using magic mapping scrolls, he found a level with a 
second stairwell up, leading to the tower of a vampire lord named Vlad. Vlad 
guarded the legendary Candlelabrum of Invocation, which Jack wrested from 
Vlad's grasp.

Further down, he met a foul beast: a master mind flayer. The creature 
attacked as he was engraving on the floor and managed to eat some of Jack's 
gray matter, but Jack assaulted him with Vorpal Blade and quickly killed 
him. Still, the damage was done, and some of the items in his inventory 
became unfamiliar, and his memory of some of the levels he had visited was 
erased. Jack still needed candles, however, and so he returned to minetown 
to buy them from a noble soul of light. He re-explored levels he had 
forgotten along the way.

He then found another necropolis, this time belonging to a foul demon of 
great power named Orcus. Deciding not to challenge him directly, Jack 
tunneled belong Orcustown and mapped the level below. He found a cockatrice, 
killed it, and put it in his bag. Returning to the up stairs, he wielded the 
corpse, went up, and turned Orcus to stone before he could react. He then 
slew his minions, chopped the statue apart and recovered his prize: a wand 
of death.

Around level 48 he discovered an odd place in the floor that vibrated. 
Returning up, he first found a false tower leading to the Wizard of Yendor's 
lair, then finally he found the true entrance. Slaying an army of monsters, 
Jack finally confronted the wizard, blasing him with his wand of death. He 
took the tome the wizard guarded: the Book of the Dead, and returned to the 
vibrating square. Jack used the items in sequence, and a passage to the 
final level of hell lay before him. Another army of undead barred his 
entrance, but they were no match for him. A number of priests waited to 
block his path, but they were quickly dispatched when he managed to slay 
another cockatrice and wield its corpse. Jack used his wand of secret door 
location to find the entrance to Moloch's sanctum, and turned the high 
priest to stone. He then procured the Amulet of Yendor and made his way up 
as best he could, but the Amulet would occasionally return him a level or 
two back.

The wizard would confront Jack twice more on the way up, and a final time on 
the Plane of Earth. Jack used a wand of cancellation to make blank scrolls, 
and wrote scrolls of gold detection which, when drunk or confused, allowed 
him to see the portal to the next plane. Jack used a wand of digging and 
quickly proceeded to the portal.

On the Plane of Air, Jack made good use of his sleep wands, putting air 
elementals who had swallowed him to sleep long enough for him to kill them. 
He quickly found the portal without incident and proceeded to the Plane of 
Fire, where he continued levitating and reached the portal to the Plane of 
Water with ease.

On the Plane of Water, Jack decided it was time to use his ring of confict 
for the upcoming final battle. But when he reached into his bag, he could 
not tell which one it was. So he used a last wish for a ring, not wanting to 
spend a lot of time writing identify scrolls to find it. Jack drifted in a 
bubble for a short while until a portal to the Astal Plane appeared.

Jack put his ring on and was almost immediately assailed by monsters. 
Several liches appeared and summoned monsters, but Jack managed to defeat 
them. Seeing that famine guarded the central altar, Jack decided to go that 
way. He had saved up a number of wands of teleportation and still had 
several charges left in his wand of death. He dispatched famine, then made 
his way to the altar, but it belonged to chaos. Very well, he supposed, I 
will try the left hand altar. Unfortunatly, however, Death had discovered 
his presence and proceeded from the right altar path down, chasing him 
towards Pestilence on the right. Jack managed to put some insects between 
himself and Pestilence but Death managed to attack, draining off about 60hp. 
He teleported a number of enemies out of the way and proceeded to the left 
altar, which belonged to the god of Law. With Pestilence and Death waiting 
outside the temple, Jack managed to push a bolder thrown by a fire giant 
into the altar entrance, and wait for the riders to back off a bit. Once 
they had, he teleported the boulder (and several enemies) away, then lead 
them on a chase around the back of the temple, where he managed to lose 
them.

Famine soon re-entered the fight but was no match for Jack's wand of death. 
The last temple soon lay before Jack, who reached the altar and finally, 
offered the Amulet of Yendor to The Lady and was rewarded with immortality. 
To make the victory even more sweet, no species were ever genocided during 
this epic quest.





